			],
			"Message": "build(Mountain): Enable experimental feature flags for terminals and debug protocol\n\nUpdate the default feature set in `Cargo.toml` to conditionally enable `terminals`, `debug-protocol`, `scm-support`, `child-processes`, and `Development` alongside existing `Cocoon`, `Mist`, and `Air` integrations. This consolidates the build configuration to activate support for terminal emulation, debugging capabilities, source control management, and child process handling by default.\n\nRemove the now-unused `grove` and `cocoon` feature placeholders from the list, aligning the feature registry with the current implementation status. This prepares the native backend for upcoming component integration while maintaining the explicit `Telemetry` and `Test` flags for optional toggling.",
			"SHA": "a2869e478f35c9bc90a23edc28e7a95f066f88a9"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-13T22:38:10Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1490,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Enable content protection for macOS overlay title bar\n\nAdd the `content_protected(true)` setting to the macOS `WindowBuilder` configuration in `WindowBuild.rs`. While the `TitleBarStyle::Overlay` is required for custom traffic light rendering, it allows webview content to extend underneath the system window controls by default.\n\nThis protection flag reserves a safe zone around the traffic-light cluster on macOS, preventing the `WKWebView` from rendering underneath the native buttons. This resolves potential click-through issues and visual overlap between the editor workbench and the system titlebar, ensuring the custom traffic lights remain interactive and visually distinct.",
			"SHA": "2d569c4b2d69bcbff098a7bcaf449f7098f3db8b"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-13T22:23:17Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1490,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Mountain): Wrap webview scheme handler closure in AssertUnwindSafe\n\nUpdate the `catch_unwind` invocation in `VscodeWebviewSchemeHandler` within `Scheme.rs` to explicitly wrap the closure in `AssertUnwindSafe`. The previous implementation omitted this trait requirement, causing compilation failures when attempting to catch panics inside the async closure passed to the panic handler.\n\nThis restores the ability to gracefully handle panics within the VS Code webview asset loading path, ensuring that errors in MIME resolution or file reading return HTTP 500 responses rather than crashing the editor process. The change aligns `VscodeWebviewSchemeHandler` with the established panic recovery pattern used in `VscodeFileSchemeHandler`.",
			"SHA": "01422c257e48f4a9f8a8fb32093daf2a7d283073"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-13T22:16:54Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1490,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Mountain): Consolidate panic recovery logic in scheme handlers\n\nMigrate the panic recovery implementation in `VscodeWebviewSchemeHandler` to match the established pattern in `VscodeFileSchemeHandler`. The previous implementation attempted to use `catch_unwind` but failed to handle the `AssertUnwindSafe` trait requirement and redundantly implemented the result matching logic inside the closure.\n\nUpdate `VscodeWebviewSchemeHandler` to use `catch_unwind` with `AssertUnwindSafe` for the closure wrapper and move the `match` result handling and `dev_log` error reporting to the function scope. This ensures that panics within the webview asset loading path (e.g., in MIME type resolution or file reading) are caught, logged with backtrace details, and converted to HTTP 500 responses without crashing the editor process.\n\nAlign the import structure in `Scheme.rs` to group `panic` imports correctly. This change unifies the error handling strategy for both file and webview scheme handlers, ensuring consistent graceful degradation across the native backend's asset serving layer.",
			"SHA": "139de360adc2c0b17a5adbef32cee1d8d6e06c54"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-13T22:10:11Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1490,
				2
			],
			"Message": "fix(Mountain): Migrate VS Code webview scheme handler to internal panic wrapper\n\nMove the VS Code webview scheme handler logic into a private `_VscodeWebviewSchemeHandler` function and wrap the public entry point with `std::panic::catch_unwind`. This mirrors the protective pattern recently implemented for the file scheme handler to prevent editor crashes caused by panics crossing the Objective-C FFI boundary.\n\nConcurrently, remove the `LSEnvironment` dictionary from `Info.plist`. These runtime configuration values (including network ports and feature tiers) were previously stored in the property list but are now generated and injected at build time via `build.rs` and `Scheme.rs`. Removing the dead XML nodes cleans up the configuration file and aligns the binary structure with the current build process.\n\nThis change ensures graceful error recovery for webview asset requests and resolves configuration drift by centralizing environment setup in the Rust build logic.",
			"SHA": "230ca02557a152875ce896802af0b4c5ec9d5929"
		}
	],
	"TimeStamp": 1779985042220
}